{#- ACHILLES TOOL, v1 — English sibling of /members/aquiles/. Same 8 routines,
    same gated block, same library structure; only the copy changes.

    Original note follows.

    ACHILLES TOOL, v1 — built Aug 13, 2026 from Iván's Achilles source doc.
    Home doc: `achilles-brief.md`. Second artifact on the strength engine.

    FOUR questions → one of 8 routines, plus one gated block:
      location (mid-portion | insertional) × place (home | gym) × roller
      + an energy-storage block shown only if the athlete says the strength
        work is already pain-free.

    *** The location axis is the reason this tool is not a copy of the knee one.
    Insertional tendinopathy is made WORSE by dorsiflexion past neutral, which
    compresses the tendon against the calcaneus — and that is exactly what a
    heel drop off a step does at the bottom of every rep. So insertional gets:
    flat ground instead of a step, range capped at neutral, capped range on the
    gym machines, and NO end-range calf stretch at all. Do not "simplify" this
    away; following the source doc as written would have had insertional
    athletes doing 45 compressive reps a session. ***

    Frequency differs by place, on Iván's call: home = Alfredson (daily, twice
    daily), gym = HSR (3×/week), with a note saying they are two roads to the
    same place. -#}
{% raw %}
<div class="setup" id="setup">
  <div class="crumb-bar"><div class="wrap"><a href="/members/en/#biblioteca">Library</a> / Achilles</div></div>

  <div class="setup-head">
    <span class="label">Strength</span>
    <h1>Pain-free Achilles</h1>
    <p>Tendons don't heal with rest. They heal with well-dosed load. This is the loading programme — and the rules for knowing, each day, whether you overdid it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-warn">
    <strong>Two things before you start.</strong><br>
    <strong>1. If you felt a snap and you cannot push off or rise onto the toes of that foot, go to urgent care today</strong> — that is not tendinopathy. <br>
    <strong>2. This starts after a professional has seen you</strong> and ruled out a partial tear. With swelling, heat or redness, or if you have recently taken fluoroquinolone antibiotics, talk it through before loading the tendon. Write to me and we'll sort it out.
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Where exactly does it hurt?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="site">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="mid" aria-pressed="true">Mid-portion<span>2-6 cm above the heel</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="ins" aria-pressed="false">Right at the bottom<span>where it meets the bone</span></button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint"><strong>Press with two fingers and find where it jumps.</strong> This is not a detail: if the pain is low, at the insertion, dropping the heel below the step crushes the tendon against the bone on every single rep. It is the most common mistake in Achilles rehab, and the routine changes to avoid it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Where are you training?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="place">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="home" aria-pressed="true">At home<span>daily protocol</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="gym" aria-pressed="false">At the gym<span>heavy load, 3×/week</span></button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint">Two roads to the same place. At home is the classic eccentric protocol: high frequency, little weight. At the gym is heavy slow resistance: three times a week, more weight, fewer sessions. The trials tie them; the gym one gets abandoned less often.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Do you have a foam roller?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="roller">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="yes" aria-pressed="true">Yes</button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="no" aria-pressed="false">No</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Is the strength work already pain-free?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="stage">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="no" aria-pressed="true">Not yet</button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="yes" aria-pressed="false">Yes, pain-free</button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint">If you get through all the strength work with no discomfort and no extra stiffness the next morning, a jumping block gets added. <strong>That is where the tendon relearns how to be a spring</strong> — and where most people reinjure themselves, by going from heavy calf raises straight back to intervals. If you are unsure, leave it on "not yet".</p>
  </div>

  <button type="button" class="btn-primary setup-go" id="setupGo">Build my routine →</button>
</div>

<div id="toolWrap" hidden>
{% endraw %}
